                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0809784d-65bf-316a-9e23-30642b20dcf0",
                        "entry_id": "d3259fd5-db5a-33d1-8707-cad2b1bd9045",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ce qu’il fallait démontrer, dans une démonstration logique ou mathématique."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b2f88b47-0924-4325-8ea4-8746ce63b14f",
                                "sense_id": "0809784d-65bf-316a-9e23-30642b20dcf0",
                                "text": "Quand on tourne une manivelle il joue un petit air aigrelet, triste et comique, saccadé comme les pas d’un vieillard, qui se termine à la façon d’un théorème par une espèce d’accord secret grêlement triomphant qui a l’air de dire CQFD."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b91e1f05-8ac7-4c68-b44e-b4ce599f743b",
                        "source_entry_id": "d3259fd5-db5a-33d1-8707-cad2b1bd9045",
                        "source_sense_id": "0809784d-65bf-316a-9e23-30642b20dcf0",
                        "target_lang_word": "q.e.d.",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "897c0467-034d-4ea6-817b-c1280910b8e1",
                        "source_entry_id": "d3259fd5-db5a-33d1-8707-cad2b1bd9045",
                        "source_sense_id": "0809784d-65bf-316a-9e23-30642b20dcf0",
                        "target_lang_word": "QED",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "QED",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                        "entry_id": "ce51b52c-033d-3bee-be1b-edbf3b9b0094",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "(Partial) initialism of quantum electrodynamics."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "59cb57da-0d73-4bbb-b1b5-ff8658940864",
                                                "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                                "text": "QCD is a theory of quark interactions much analogous to QED: the interaction is carried by \"gluons\" (analogous to photons) which couple to the \"color\" (analogous to charge) of the quarks."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "92c391d0-1f02-4d70-a441-eb248d2c37a3",
                                                "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                                "text": "By the way, these days QED is considered a relatively simple example of a quantum field theory."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d28a2756-3627-41a3-918b-60c153e1fc7d",
                                                "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                                "text": "QED is the theory that explains how electrically charged particles, like electrons, interact with each other and with particles of light (photons). […] Pretty much everything else – certainly everything you see and feel around you – is explained at the deepest known level by QED. Matter, light, electricity and magnetism – it is all QED."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c1591e6-cc67-4fdc-8783-a441126b008a",
                                                "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                                "text": "Finally, there is still plenty of room to employ and apply QED theory for predictive purposes, by proposing new phenomena, especially within the realm of photonics, thereby ensuring QED remains relevant to current and future generations of researchers working in chemical physics."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9107682c-0651-42b7-ab05-740327ede7a3",
                                                "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                                "text": "The aim of this work is essentially twofold: to establish the conception and thus model of a 'unitary universal cohesive field' from 'first principles' within which existing theories, primarily QED and the foundation of its approach, may be understood both in principle and therefore from any abstruse mathematical perspective extrapolated from it; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6b824b0c-49d7-4f0d-9eed-913c302ce95d",
                                                "sense_id": "b16af37f-6557-3493-91fa-d277b1d93079",
                                                "text": "Although his own early work on QED helped bring photons and electrons into a consistent framework, Dr. Dyson doubted that superstrings, or anything else, would lead to a Theory of Everything, unifying all of physics with a succinct formulation inscribable on a T-shirt."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "QED",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "be0adbc8-f8b4-3d3f-bd7c-c35b486b0974",
                                        "entry_id": "ef8a00c6-a56f-316b-ac84-346d842a39b8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of Q.E.D.; Initialism of quod erat demonstrandum."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6698ff69-fe23-41a4-9b94-7fddcce167fd",
                                                "sense_id": "be0adbc8-f8b4-3d3f-bd7c-c35b486b0974",
                                                "text": "[A]fter the ſame manner S and U are proved to be equal, therefore the ſquare of CB is equal to the ſquare of the 2 other ſides QED."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3d1cfb39-dc1c-495f-a351-30fbcb36ca21",
                        "source_entry_id": "d3259fd5-db5a-33d1-8707-cad2b1bd9045",
                        "source_sense_id": "0809784d-65bf-316a-9e23-30642b20dcf0",
                        "target_lang_word": "Q.E.D.",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Q.E.D.",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "643bb16f-1b3a-360a-a3b3-627a03bcf4b3",
                                        "entry_id": "7c3b0504-8b62-348a-9fd9-c27abbe21840",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Initialism of quod erat demonstrandum (“what was to be proved; what was to be demonstrated”): placed at the end of a mathematical proof to show that the theorem under discussion is proved."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "abbreviation",
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "dated",
                                            "initialism"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "22ad3fb3-ea24-4a5c-8d3c-99ef6352b139",
                                                "sense_id": "643bb16f-1b3a-360a-a3b3-627a03bcf4b3",
                                                "text": "[A]fter the same manner S and U are proved to be equal, therefore the square of CB is equal to the square of the 2 other sides Q E D."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "572c5762-df30-489d-a320-b5127296bac4",
                                                "sense_id": "643bb16f-1b3a-360a-a3b3-627a03bcf4b3",
                                                "text": "Now let any right line meet four harmonicals in A, C, B, D, and if those harmonicals be parallel, the thing is evident; but, if they intersect in V, draw ECF parallel to VD, the line most remote from C; then, by the first part, EC#61;CF, and AD#58;AC#58;#58;VD#58;EC or CF. Therefore AD#58;AC#58;#58;BD#58;CB. q. e. d."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0f78fc9f-418a-4792-9a6a-2b5bc51672c0",
                                                "sense_id": "643bb16f-1b3a-360a-a3b3-627a03bcf4b3",
                                                "text": "By Cases 1 and 2, we see that any finite collection of 4k-1 primes cannot contain all such primes. Thus there are infinitely many primes of this type. ¶ Q.E.D."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0ee8f2b0-b1f5-3b97-aad4-695ac8fba19c",
                                        "entry_id": "7c3b0504-8b62-348a-9fd9-c27abbe21840",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Used to indicate that an argument or proposition is proved by the existence of some fact or scenario."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a957421a-e433-4293-997b-a390555c1f2b",
                                                "sense_id": "0ee8f2b0-b1f5-3b97-aad4-695ac8fba19c",
                                                "text": "That this part of the world has actually been peopled (Q. E. D.) to support which, we have living proofs in the numerous tribes of Indians that inhabit it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1e28de6c-e687-4c6c-ba1d-bf123828abe7",
                                                "sense_id": "0ee8f2b0-b1f5-3b97-aad4-695ac8fba19c",
                                                "text": "PROP[OSITION] VI. One substance cannot be produced by another substance. ¶ Demonst[ration]. In the preceding proposition we have seen that there cannot in the nature of things be two Substances of the same attribute, or that they have anything in common (by Prop. II.); and so (by Prop. III.) one cannot be the cause of, or be produced by, another: q. e. d."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "767670a9-3e25-45ef-84f7-9a68bc72e49c",
                                                "sense_id": "0ee8f2b0-b1f5-3b97-aad4-695ac8fba19c",
                                                "text": "Of all those on the market, Eastman's is the only one with a solution tight, locking cover. ¶ Q. E. D."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "Q.E.D.",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d8f1ed9c-c0de-3e25-8151-c8943439f8ac",
                                        "entry_id": "74808c2d-3d38-37df-be4a-44754982c9e5",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A certain fact or scenario that proves an argument or proposition; a justification."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2a58466f-16d6-4050-942a-ad5f11d405d4",
                                                "sense_id": "d8f1ed9c-c0de-3e25-8151-c8943439f8ac",
                                                "text": "We all go in for the dolce here except you, and you're such a patent machine for turning out Q.E.D.s by the dozen, that you can no more help working than the bedmaker can help taking my tea and saying the cat did it,[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "420dec94-b2e3-46c3-ba3d-8c40739d8353",
                                                "sense_id": "d8f1ed9c-c0de-3e25-8151-c8943439f8ac",
                                                "text": "The same thing, I believe, that was lacking in Let Freedom Ring—lack of dramatic integration, lack of dramatic intensity, lack of all those elements, call them what you will, which make a play a play and not a tract, and certainly not a Q.E.D. thesis."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
